 "narrative": "The new conditional and interaction results sharpen the structure of the three validated dataset axes while also introducing one unresolved effect-modification question. All due comparisons had valid Welch cells and returned estimates are already signed to the claimed hypothesis direction.\n\nH84-H87 are strong evidence that feature_037 and feature_010 retain their accepted associations after conditioning on feature_002. Within feature_002=0, feature_037=1 is associated with lower outcome_001 values, i.e. stronger composite dependency, by +0.832 with 95% CI +0.771 to +0.893 (R64). Within feature_002=1, the same association remains +0.809 with CI +0.678 to +0.940 (R65). Feature_010=1 is associated with higher outcome_001 values, i.e. weaker dependency, by +0.367 within feature_002=0 (CI +0.265 to +0.468; R66) and +0.576 within feature_002=1 (CI +0.414 to +0.739; R67). All four lower bounds are far above the 0.15-unit threshold, so I accept these exact dataset-internal subgroup claims and close them. This argues against simple collapse: feature_002 is not merely carrying the feature_037 association, and feature_037/feature_010 are not merely proxies for feature_002.\n\nH88 asks whether the large feature_002 weaker-dependency/higher-outcome association is larger among feature_010=1 than among its complement. The signed interaction estimate is +0.210 composite dependency-score units, CI +0.019 to +0.400 (R68). The point estimate exceeds the 0.15-unit threshold and the interval excludes zero in the claimed direction, which is promising, but the lower bound is below 0.15. Given the number of exploratory comparisons and the fact that this is an interaction rather than a central main effect, the discovery evidence is not sufficient to accept a threshold-exceeding effect modification claim. It is also not a clear rejection because the interval extends well above threshold. I assess H88 as unresolved and keep it active. This is the most informative candidate fo