nty is the boundary and attribution of the feature_010 interaction pattern. Several nested cutoff claims are accepted on discovery evidence, including high-feature_018 and high-feature_038 attenuation at various tails, but feature_038 and feature_018 are constructed correlated assays. H120 validates a broad feature_018 tail contrast, but it does not prove that feature_018 independently modifies outcome_001 once feature_038 is accounted for, nor does it prove the attenuation boundary is exactly at zero versus another cutoff. The newly registered proposals address this directly by testing feature_018 attenuation conditional on feature_038 being nonnegative and feature_038 attenuation conditional on feature_018 being nonnegative, plus cross-stratum interactions that require one assay to be high while the other is low. If those lower bounds clear the 0.15 threshold in the claimed direction, the attenuation pattern can be interpreted as less tied to one extreme cutoff or one assay alone. If they fail, the validated claims should remain narrowly tied to the tested restricted tails and eligibility rules.\n\nThe most useful next analyses are therefore the new conditional tail-interaction tests together with the still-untested central direct interactions for feature_037-by-feature_010 and the joint-stratum persistence analyses for feature_002, feature_010, and feature_037. If independent validation remains available after appraisal, the best candidates are the conditional tests that most cleanly separate feature_038 from feature_018, or H111/H119 as unvalidated boundary refinements. All conclusions remain dataset-internal association claims: they do not establish causality, biological mechanism, or external generalizability.",
